{
  "id": "azuresql-db-import",
  "domain": "azure-sql-database-container",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data",
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption",
    "multi-step-with-silent-failures"
  ],
  "correction": "Asked to get an existing database into the local container, an agent reaches for a backup file and RESTORE DATABASE, because that is how a database gets into SQL Server. There is no restore path here. This is the Azure SQL Database engine, BACKUP and RESTORE are refused in every session with Msg 40510, and the supported route is SqlPackage against a .bacpac for schema and data or a .dacpac for schema alone. Two more beliefs turn that route into wasted time. The first is that every action creates its target. /Action:Import does not, so the database has to exist and be empty on a master connection before SqlPackage is pointed at it. /Action:Publish does create the database when it is absent, which is why the .dacpac route behaves differently from the .bacpac route. Either way the tool must target that user database rather than master. The second is that a successful exit means a faithful copy. This engine does not carry SQL Server Agent jobs, server-scoped logins, linked servers, filestream, explicit filegroup placement or cross-database three-part-name references, and an import that meets them reports them in its log rather than in its exit path, so an unread log is the difference between a database that matches the source and one that merely finished importing. SqlPackage itself is a host tool installed with dotnet tool install; the image does not ship it, so the command belongs on the developer's machine and not inside a docker exec.",
  "value_declaration": {
    "rationale": "This skill's kernel is that the route an agent reaches for does not exist here, and that two actions of the replacement tool differ in whether they create the target. The Azure SQL Database container is a gated private preview and the image is not publicly available, so no model has training data on the product, and the arm of a two-arm value run that ran without this skill would fail for the least interesting reason available: it has never heard of the thing. Running that measurement across all seventeen container skills would spend around a thousand model requests to re-establish what the product's release status already establishes. Carlos Robles accepted value for this collection on 2026-09-04 on this written argument instead. THIS IS A DECLARATION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT, and nothing may report it as one. The argument reaches only the container-specific corrections listed in covers, which are the ones no training data contains. It does not reach SqlPackage command-line syntax and the bacpac and dacpac formats, which are publicly documented, which is ordinary material an ordinary model already handles, and whether this skill improves an answer there is unmeasured and stays unmeasured. The argument ends when the Azure SQL Database container reaches Public Preview: training data begins to contain the product, the premise that nothing could know it stops being true, and this skill has to be measured or demoted.",
    "covers": [
      "there being no restore path on this engine, with BACKUP and RESTORE refused with Msg 40510",
      "/Action:Import not creating its target, so the database must exist and be empty first, where /Action:Publish does create it",
      "SqlPackage not being present inside the image, so it runs from the host against the published port",
      "USE being refused with Msg 40508 during provisioning"
    ],
    "does_not_cover": [
      "SqlPackage command-line syntax and the bacpac and dacpac formats",
      "general data-migration sequencing advice"
    ],
    "decided_by": "Carlos Robles",
    "decided_on": "2026-09-04",
    "expires_when": "the-container-reaches-public-preview"
  },
  "posture": [
    "provision",
    "write",
    "execute"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db-container"
  ],
  "related": [
    "azuresql-db-container",
    "azuresql-db-from-sql-server",
    "azuresql-db-schema-migration"
  ],
  "value_probe": {
    "task": "We have a copy of the production database and I want it running in the Azure SQL Database container on my machine so I can debug against real data instead of fixtures. Walk me through getting it in, and tell me how I know it actually arrived intact.",
    "gate": "The answer must route to SqlPackage against a .bacpac or .dacpac run from the host, and must say plainly that there is no restore path on this engine rather than offering a .bak workaround.",
    "states": [
      "BACKUP and RESTORE are refused in every session with Msg 40510, matching Azure SQL Database in the cloud, so a .bak is not a route in.",
      "The supported route is SqlPackage: /Action:Import for a .bacpac carrying schema and data, /Action:Publish for a .dacpac carrying schema alone.",
      "The target database has to exist and be empty before /Action:Import is pointed at it, created on a master connection, and SqlPackage targets that user database rather than master.",
      "SqlPackage is a host tool, installed with dotnet tool install -g microsoft.sqlpackage, and is not in the container image, so the command runs on the developer's machine against the mapped host port.",
      "This engine carries no SQL Server Agent jobs, no server-scoped logins, no linked servers, no filestream, no explicit filegroup placement and no cross-database three-part-name references, and an import that meets any of them reports it in the log.",
      "The import log is read before the copy is called faithful, because the objects that did not come across are named there and not in the exit code."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "That RESTORE DATABASE ... FROM DISK works once the .bak is copied into the container. It is refused with Msg 40510, and there is no restore path at all.",
      "That the container is SQL Server underneath, so the usual backup and restore workflow applies with a different path.",
      "That SqlPackage /Action:Import creates the database it is pointed at. It does not, and the import fails until an empty database exists.",
      "That SqlPackage can be run with docker exec inside the container. The image does not ship it.",
      "That SqlPackage should be pointed at master and told the database name. It targets the user database directly.",
      "That a zero exit code means a faithful copy. Unsupported objects are reported in the log while the run still finishes, so an unread log is the difference between a database that matches the source and one that merely finished importing."
    ]
  },
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition, CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS nvarchar(128)) AS edition;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              5,
              "SQL Azure"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The self-check the skill puts in front of every other instruction. Engine edition 5 with edition 'SQL Azure' is the Azure SQL Database engine; the SQL Server image mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server returns different values, so any other answer means the lane ran against the wrong engine and every probe below it is measuring something else."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40508
        },
        "why": "The skill states that in a user-database session USE returns Msg 40508, exactly as in Azure SQL Database in the cloud, and that the fix is always to select the database in the connection string. Every probe here runs against a scratch user database, which is the session where the Azure statement filter is enforced. If this ever succeeds, the filter is not being applied to the lane's session and the skill's central connection-model rule has stopped being true locally."
      },
      {
        "sql": "DECLARE @stmt nvarchar(max) = N'RESTORE DATABASE ' + QUOTENAME(DB_NAME()) + N' FROM DISK = ''/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_restore.bak''';\nEXEC (@stmt);",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40510
        },
        "why": "This skill exists because there is no restore path on this engine, and an agent's first instinct is a .bak file. The azuresql-db-container skill records BACKUP and RESTORE as unsupported in any session with Msg 40510, the number that refuses a whole statement rather than one option of a supported one, and matching Azure SQL Database in the cloud. The database name is built from DB_NAME() so the refusal is measured against the scratch database this lane holds. If this ever stops being 40510, the recommendation to reach for SqlPackage stops being forced and the skill should say so."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "SqlPackage /version",
        "where": "host",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "SqlPackage is a host tool, installed with dotnet tool install -g microsoft.sqlpackage, and the skill's import commands are written to run on the developer's machine against the mapped host port. Declaring host here is the point of the probe: five probes elsewhere in this corpus shipped without the field and every one failed with command not found, because they were written by running the tool on a laptop and then executed inside a container. A red here means the lane's host has no SqlPackage installed, which is a finding about the lane rather than about the engine."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "test ! -e /opt/sqlpackage",
        "where": "container",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "references/sqlpackage-import.md states plainly that the container image does not ship SqlPackage and that the simplest supported path is to install it on the host. This is the container half of that claim, and it is evidence rather than proof: a future image could ship the tool at some other path and this probe would still pass. Read a red here as the image having gained a bundled SqlPackage, which would make the reference's container-based fallback section wrong in a useful direction."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Import this bacpac into the local container",
      "Publish my dacpac to the container database",
      "Load my existing production database into the local engine",
      "Bring the prod schema down into my dev database",
      "What SqlPackage flags do I need to import into the container"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "I have a .bak of the database, how do I get it running locally",
      "SqlPackage says the target database does not exist",
      "The import finished but half my objects are missing",
      "How do I get my real data into the local engine so I can debug against it",
      "My import fails on a statement that references another database by three-part name"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "Generate a thousand rows of fake data for the empty tables",
      "Write an EF Core migration for this model change",
      "Create a least-privilege user for the application to connect as",
      "Set up connection retry for transient faults",
      "Start the container and give me a connection string"
    ]
  }
}
